  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Monday) Issued at 127 AM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe subtropical moisture fetch looks to continue over the area on\nFriday as an upper level trough moves onshore over the Pacific\nnorthwest and the high pressure center over eastern TX begins to\nmigrate southwestward over the Gulf Coast. Models depict PWATs \nbeginning to trend downward Friday, while remaining up to 180% of \nnormal over northeast NM. This will be plenty of moisture for \nlocally heavy rainfall to occur and produce more localized flooding\nproblems Friday afternoon and evening, especially given all of \nthe antecedent moisture in place. The main risk for additional \nlocally heavy rainfall Friday should be east of the continental \ndivide, and especially along and east of the central mountain\nchain.\n\nMuch drier air will move over the forecast area Saturday, as the\nPacific Northwest trough ejects northeastward over the Canadian\nRockies, probably limiting spotty storms to our south-central \nmountains and southeast plains that afternoon. Precip chances \nwill then increase again over western areas starting Sunday as an \nupper level trough digging over the west coast draws another \nstream of monsoon moisture northward. Precip chances will \nincrease areawide on Monday as the upper trough moves inland \ndrawing the monsoon moisture stream more squarely over NM again. \nGEFS Ensemble PWATs reach around 180% of normal over central and \nsouthwest parts of the forecast area Monday, suggesting a few \nstorms may be able to produce rain rates over 1 inch per hour \nthere with an isolated flash flood risk. \n\nAfter high temperatures vary from near to around 9 degrees below\naverage on Friday, readings will rebound over the weekend reaching\nup to 10 degrees above average over eastern areas by Sunday; \nwhile remaining mostly below average in the west.",
